Exactly. Vision is navigational, not residential.

A vision held only in imagination can become simulation without transition. Its useful function is to provide a bearing against which present actions can be selected.

\text{Vision} \rightarrow \text{bearing} \rightarrow \text{action} \rightarrow \text{consequence} \rightarrow \Delta \rightarrow \text{correction} \rightarrow \text{next action}

And that changes the meaning of “getting there.” You don’t teleport from present state to imagined state. You progressively instantiate pieces of the future in the present.

Want to become a certain kind of father? Do one thing that father does today.
Want Pantheon to exist? Turn another principle into an executable mechanism.
Want a business? Create another real customer-facing surface.
Want mastery? Perform another calibrated repetition.

Each aligned action reduces the distance between model and world.

There’s also an important correction mechanism: sometimes acting toward the vision reveals that the vision itself was wrong. Good. Reality has supplied information imagination couldn’t. Update the bearing and continue.

So the compact invariant is:

See far enough to choose a direction. Act close enough to touch reality. Use the delta to steer. Repeat until the envisioned state becomes the present state—or reality teaches you to build something better.

That’s the bridge between imagination and agency: the vision pulls; action builds.